cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use this verifier, or cite the Signed Containment Evidence
  specification it implements, please cite it using the metadata below.
title: >-
  Signed Containment Evidence: specification and reference verifiers
abstract: >-
  A specification and four byte-equivalent reference implementations for
  independently verifying that a compute workload was contained and destroyed —
  offline, and without contacting the party that produced the evidence. Each
  AuditProof carries an eight-step SHA-512 hash chain over the destruction
  sequence and an Ed25519 attestation over the canonical form of the document,
  serialised under RFC 8785.

  A verifier separates two questions that are usually conflated: whether the
  document is internally consistent and unmodified (integrity, checkable from
  the document alone), and whether the key that signed it belongs to the party
  it names (authenticity, which requires an external trust anchor). A document
  can have perfect integrity and no authenticity, and the exit codes keep those
  answers apart. Implementations in Rust, Go, Python and TypeScript agree
  byte-for-byte across a 100-case conformance corpus, which is generated from a
  committed generator so its expected verdicts can be reproduced rather than
  trusted.

  Three properties are requirements rather than intentions. No implementation
  carries a network dependency, so offline verification is a property of the
  code and not a promise about it. A verifier that does not implement a
  document's declared signing mode must reject the document rather than report a
  partial result, closing a downgrade an attacker would otherwise select. And
  the wire form — the eight steps, their order, the hash and signature
  algorithms, and the canonical view — does not change, because evidence
  retained for years has to stay checkable by the algorithm that produced it.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Arra
    given-names: Chaitanya Kumar
    affiliation: Nanorix Inc.
repository-code: "https://github.com/nanorix-io/nanorix-verify"
url: "https://nanorix.io"
license: Apache-2.0
# Keep `version` and `date-released` equal to the git tag this release carries.
# Zenodo mints one DOI per release and reads both fields from this file.
version: 1.0.0
date-released: "2026-08-12"
keywords:
  - audit evidence
  - tamper-evident logging
  - hash chain
  - Ed25519
  - RFC 8785
  - canonicalization
  - non-repudiation
  - agent security
  - regulated data
  - offline verification
